window.A.registerBank({"chapter": 8, "title": "Coordinate geometry of the circle", "syllabusOutcomes": {"8.1": "Know and use the equation of a circle: (x−a)²+(y−b)²=r² and x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c=0; identify centre and radius from any form", "8.2": "Solve problems on the intersection of a circle and a straight line (tangent / chord / no intersection)", "8.3": "Solve problems involving tangents to a circle (no calculus expected)", "8.4": "Solve problems on the intersection of two circles (points of intersection, common chord, touch / do not intersect)"}, "groups": [{"key": "basics", "label": "Centre, radius & the circle equation", "outcomes": ["8.1"]}, {"key": "line", "label": "Circle meets line: tangents & chords", "outcomes": ["8.2", "8.3"]}, {"key": "build", "label": "Constructing circles from conditions", "outcomes": ["8.1", "8.2"]}, {"key": "two", "label": "Two circles", "outcomes": ["8.4"]}], "questions": [{"id": "s26-p11-q2", "group": "basics", "source": {"session": "May/June 2026", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "2"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["8.1"], "topics": ["centre & radius", "diameter endpoint"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 1, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "A circle has equation \\((x-3)^2 + (y-4)^2 = 20\\). Write down the coordinates of the centre of the circle.", "marks": 1}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Write down the radius of the circle.", "marks": 1}, {"label": "(c)", "text": "The line AB is a diameter of this circle. The point A has coordinates \\((-1, 6)\\). Find the coordinates of the point B.", "marks": 2}], "statement": "<p>A circle has equation \\(\\;(x-3)^2 + (y-4)^2 = 20\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> Write down the coordinates of the centre of the circle.&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Write down the radius of the circle.&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p><p><b>(c)</b> The line \\(AB\\) is a diameter of this circle. The point \\(A\\) has coordinates \\((-1, 6)\\). Find the coordinates of the point \\(B\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p>", "tips": ["Read centre and radius straight from \\((x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2\\): centre \\((a,b)\\) — flip the signs — and \\(r=\\sqrt{r^2}\\).", "\\(\\sqrt{20}\\) simplifies: \\(\\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt5\\). Leave radii exact.", "The centre is the MIDPOINT of a diameter: \\(B = 2C - A\\) (double the centre, subtract A — coordinate by coordinate)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Centre \\((3, 4)\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(r = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt5\\).</p>", "<p><b>(c)</b> The centre is the midpoint of \\(AB\\): \\(B = (2\\cdot3 - (-1),\\; 2\\cdot4 - 6) = (7, 2)\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-3, 9], "y": [-2, 10], "xTicks": 2, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"circle": [3, 4, 4.4721]}], "points": [[3, 4, "C(3,4)"], [-1, 6, "A(−1,6)"], [7, 2, "B(7,2)"]]}}], "answer": "(a) \\((3,4)\\); (b) \\(2\\sqrt5\\); (c) \\(B(7, 2)\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — \\((3, 4)\\)", "(b) B1 — \\(\\sqrt{20}\\) or \\(2\\sqrt5\\) isw", "(c) B2 — \\((7, 2)\\) (B1 for each coordinate; allow vector method)"]}, {"id": "s25-p11-q3", "group": "line", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "3"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["8.1", "8.3"], "topics": ["point on circle", "diameter endpoint", "tangent at a point"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "Point A has coordinates \\((3, -1)\\). A circle has equation \\((x-4)^2 + (y+3)^2 = 5\\). Show that A lies on the circumference of the circle.", "marks": 1}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Given that AB is a diameter of the circle, find the coordinates of B.", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(c)", "text": "Find the equation of the tangent to the circle at A.", "marks": 3}], "statement": "<p>Point \\(A\\) has coordinates \\((3, -1)\\). A circle has equation \\(\\;(x-4)^2 + (y+3)^2 = 5\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> Show that \\(A\\) lies on the circumference of the circle.&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Given that \\(AB\\) is a diameter of the circle, find the coordinates of \\(B\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(c)</b> Find the equation of the tangent to the circle at \\(A\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) \"Show on the circle\" = substitute A into the left side and show you get exactly \\(r^2\\): \\((3-4)^2+(-1+3)^2 = 1+4 = 5\\) ✓.", "(b) Centre \\((4,-3)\\) is the midpoint of AB: \\(B = 2C - A\\).", "(c) The tangent at a point is PERPENDICULAR to the radius there (no calculus!): gradient of radius \\(CA\\), then negative reciprocal, then line through A.", "Gradient of \\(CA\\): \\(\\frac{-1-(-3)}{3-4} = \\frac{2}{-1} = -2\\) → tangent gradient \\(= \\frac12\\)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\((3-4)^2 + (-1+3)^2 = 1 + 4 = 5 = r^2\\), so A is on the circle. ✓</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> Centre \\(C(4,-3)\\); \\(B = (2\\cdot4-3,\\; 2(-3)-(-1)) = (5, -5)\\).</p>", "<p><b>(c)</b> Gradient of radius \\(CA = \\dfrac{-1+3}{3-4} = -2\\). Tangent \\(\\perp\\) radius → gradient \\(\\tfrac12\\).</p>", "<p>Tangent at \\(A(3,-1)\\): \\(y + 1 = \\tfrac12(x - 3)\\), i.e. \\(y = \\tfrac12 x - \\tfrac52\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-1, 9], "y": [-7, 3], "xTicks": 2, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"circle": [4, -3, 2.2361]}, {"poly": [0.5, -2.5], "cls": "c1"}], "points": [[4, -3, "C"], [3, -1, "A"], [5, -5, "B"]]}}], "answer": "(b) \\(B(5,-5)\\); (c) \\(y + 1 = \\tfrac12(x-3)\\) oe", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — \\((3-4)^2+(-1+3)^2 = 5\\) (or distance from A to centre \\(=\\sqrt5=\\) radius)", "(b) M1 — midpoint/vector method with centre; A1 — \\((5, -5)\\)", "(c) M1 — gradient of radius \\(= -2\\) soi; M1 — tangent gradient \\(= -1/\\text{their}(-2)\\); A1 — \\(y+1=\\frac12(x-3)\\) oe (ISW from correct unsimplified)"]}, {"id": "s25-p12-q3", "group": "line", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "3"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["8.1", "8.2", "8.3"], "topics": ["general form", "show tangent (discriminant)", "point of contact", "build new circle"], "marks": 8, "targetMinutes": 12, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "A circle with centre C has the equation \\(x^2+y^2-10x-4y+24=0\\). Show that the line \\(y=2x-3\\) is a tangent to this circle.", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Given that this tangent touches the circle at the point P, find the coordinates of P.", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(c)", "text": "Find the equation of the circle which has its centre at P and passes through the origin.", "marks": 3}], "statement": "<p>A circle with centre \\(C\\) has the equation \\(\\;x^2+y^2-10x-4y+24=0\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> Show that the line \\(y=2x-3\\) is a tangent to this circle.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Given that this tangent touches the circle at the point \\(P\\), find the coordinates of \\(P\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(c)</b> Find the equation of the circle which has its centre at \\(P\\) and passes through the origin.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "tips": ["General form → centre/radius: \\(x^2+y^2-10x-4y+24=0\\) → centre \\((5,2)\\) (halve and flip \\(-10, -4\\)), \\(r^2 = 5^2+2^2-24 = 5\\).", "(a) \"Show tangent\": substitute the line into the circle and show the quadratic has a REPEATED root (discriminant \\(=0\\)). State the conclusion!", "(b) The point of contact is the repeated root: \\(x = \\frac{-b}{2a}\\) of your quadratic, then \\(y\\) from the line.", "(c) New circle: centre P, radius = distance from P to the origin. Radius² is all you need — don't take the square root."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Substitute \\(y=2x-3\\): \\(x^2+(2x-3)^2-10x-4(2x-3)+24=0\\)</p>", "<p>\\(x^2+4x^2-12x+9-10x-8x+12+24=0 \\Rightarrow 5x^2-30x+45=0 \\Rightarrow x^2-6x+9=0\\).</p>", "<p>Discriminant: \\((-6)^2-4(9) = 0\\) — repeated root, so the line is a tangent. ✓</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(x^2-6x+9=(x-3)^2=0 \\Rightarrow x=3\\), \\(y=2(3)-3=3\\): \\(P(3,3)\\).</p>", "<p><b>(c)</b> Radius² \\(= OP^2 = 3^2+3^2 = 18\\). Circle: \\((x-3)^2+(y-3)^2=18\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-2, 10], "y": [-3, 9], "xTicks": 2, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"circle": [5, 2, 2.2361]}, {"circle": [3, 3, 4.2426], "cls": "c2"}, {"poly": [2, -3], "cls": "c1"}], "points": [[5, 2, "C"], [3, 3, "P"], [0, 0, "O"]]}}], "answer": "(b) \\(P(3,3)\\); (c) \\((x-3)^2+(y-3)^2=18\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) M1 — substitutes and reaches \\(5x^2-30x+45=0\\) oe 3-term quadratic; A1 — discriminant \\(900-900=0\\) (or repeated root shown) with conclusion 'tangent' (alt: perpendicular distance from centre \\((5,2)\\) to the line \\(=\\sqrt5=\\) radius)", "(b) B2 — \\((3, 3)\\) (B1 each coordinate)", "(c) B1 — radius \\(=3\\sqrt2\\) or \\(r^2=18\\) soi; B1 — \\((x-3)^2+(y-3)^2\\) soi; B1 — \\(=18\\) (ISW any expansion)"]}, {"id": "w25-p12-q7", "group": "build", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "7"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["8.1"], "alsoChapters": [7], "topics": ["circle through 3 points", "right angle in semicircle"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "The point A has coordinates \\((-2,4)\\), B has coordinates \\((6,10)\\) and C has coordinates \\((12,2)\\). Find the gradients of the lines AB, AC and BC.", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Hence find the equation of the circle which passes through the points A, B and C.", "marks": 4}], "statement": "<p>The point \\(A\\) has coordinates \\((-2, 4)\\).<br>The point \\(B\\) has coordinates \\((6, 10)\\).<br>The point \\(C\\) has coordinates \\((12, 2)\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> Find the gradients of the lines \\(AB\\), \\(AC\\) and \\(BC\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> <i>Hence</i> find the equation of the circle which passes through the points \\(A\\), \\(B\\) and \\(C\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) Three straightforward gradient calculations — but the examiner chose these points for a reason…", "Spot it: \\(m_{AB}\\times m_{BC} = \\tfrac34 \\times \\left(-\\tfrac43\\right) = -1\\) — AB ⊥ BC, the angle at B is \\(90°\\).", "The angle in a semicircle is \\(90°\\) — so \\(AC\\) is a DIAMETER. That is what \"hence\" means here.", "Centre = midpoint of AC; radius² = (half of AC)² — use the centre to one endpoint, squared. No simultaneous equations needed!"], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\(m_{AB} = \\dfrac{10-4}{6+2} = \\dfrac68 = \\dfrac34\\); \\(m_{AC} = \\dfrac{2-4}{12+2} = -\\dfrac{2}{14} = -\\dfrac17\\); \\(m_{BC} = \\dfrac{2-10}{12-6} = -\\dfrac86 = -\\dfrac43\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(m_{AB}\\cdot m_{BC} = \\tfrac34\\cdot\\left(-\\tfrac43\\right) = -1\\), so \\(\\angle ABC = 90^\\circ\\). By the angle-in-a-semicircle theorem, \\(AC\\) is a diameter.</p>", "<p>Centre = midpoint of \\(AC\\) = \\(\\left(\\tfrac{-2+12}{2}, \\tfrac{4+2}{2}\\right) = (5, 3)\\).</p>", "<p>\\(r^2 = (5-(-2))^2 + (3-4)^2 = 49 + 1 = 50\\).</p>", "<p>Circle: \\((x-5)^2 + (y-3)^2 = 50\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-6, 14], "y": [-6, 12], "xTicks": 4, "yTicks": 4, "curves": [{"circle": [5, 3, 7.0711]}], "points": [[-2, 4, "A"], [6, 10, "B"], [12, 2, "C"], [5, 3, "(5,3)"]]}}], "answer": "(a) \\(\\tfrac34, -\\tfrac17, -\\tfrac43\\); (b) \\((x-5)^2+(y-3)^2=50\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B2 — all three gradients (B1 for 2 correct unsimplified)", "(b) B1 — centre \\((5,3)\\) (FT their diameter; from \\(m_{AB}m_{BC}=-1\\) ⇒ AC diameter)", "(b) M1 — uses their centre and A, B or C to find \\(r^2\\) (or halves length AC); A1 — \\(r^2=50\\)", "(b) A1 — \\((x-5)^2+(y-3)^2=50\\) oe (allow \\((5\\sqrt2)^2\\))"]}, {"id": "w25-p13-q11", "group": "build", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/13", "question": "11"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["8.1", "8.2"], "topics": ["circle from tangent lines", "show that + discriminant", "exact surds"], "marks": 7, "targetMinutes": 11, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "The lines \\(x=0\\), \\(x=4\\), \\(y=3\\) and \\(y=-1\\) are tangents to a circle. Find the equation of the circle.", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "The line \\(y=2x+a\\), where \\(a\\) is a constant, is also a tangent to the circle. Show that \\(5x^2+4(a-2)x+(a-1)^2=0\\), and hence find the possible values of \\(a\\). Give your answers in exact form.", "marks": 4}], "statement": "<p>The lines \\(x=0\\), \\(x=4\\), \\(y=3\\) and \\(y=-1\\) are tangents to a circle.</p><p><b>(a)</b> Find the equation of the circle.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p>The line \\(y=2x+a\\), where \\(a\\) is a constant, is also a tangent to the circle.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Show that \\(\\;5x^2+4(a-2)x+(a-1)^2=0\\), and hence find the possible values of \\(a\\). Give your answers in exact form.&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) Four tangent lines box the circle in: the centre is midway between \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=4\\) AND midway between \\(y=-1\\) and \\(y=3\\); the radius is half the gap.", "(b) \"Show that\": substitute \\(y=2x+a\\) into YOUR circle equation and expand carefully to the printed quadratic — every step visible.", "Tangent ⟺ discriminant \\(=0\\): \\([4(a-2)]^2 - 4\\cdot5\\cdot(a-1)^2 = 0\\).", "\"Exact form\" → surds: simplify \\(\\sqrt{80} = 4\\sqrt5\\), so \\(a = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt5\\)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Centre: \\(x = \\tfrac{0+4}{2} = 2\\), \\(y = \\tfrac{-1+3}{2} = 1\\); radius \\(= 2\\). Circle: \\((x-2)^2+(y-1)^2=4\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> Substitute \\(y = 2x+a\\): \\((x-2)^2 + (2x+a-1)^2 = 4\\)</p>", "<p>\\(x^2-4x+4 + 4x^2 + 4(a-1)x + (a-1)^2 = 4 \\Rightarrow 5x^2 + [4(a-1)-4]x + (a-1)^2 = 0 \\Rightarrow 5x^2+4(a-2)x+(a-1)^2=0\\) ✓ (AG)</p>", "<p>Tangent → discriminant zero: \\(16(a-2)^2 - 20(a-1)^2 = 0 \\Rightarrow 4(a-2)^2 = 5(a-1)^2\\)</p>", "<p>\\(4a^2-16a+16 = 5a^2-10a+5 \\Rightarrow a^2+6a-11=0 \\Rightarrow a = \\dfrac{-6\\pm\\sqrt{36+44}}{2} = -3 \\pm 2\\sqrt5\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-2, 6], "y": [-3, 5], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 1, "curves": [{"circle": [2, 1, 2]}, {"poly": [2, 1.4721], "dashed": true, "cls": "c1"}, {"poly": [2, -7.4721], "dashed": true, "cls": "c1"}], "vlines": [0, 4], "hlines": [3, -1], "points": [[2, 1, "(2,1)"]]}}], "answer": "(a) \\((x-2)^2+(y-1)^2=4\\); (b) \\(a = -3+2\\sqrt5\\) or \\(a=-3-2\\sqrt5\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — centre \\((2,1)\\); B1 — radius 2 soi; B1 — \\((x-2)^2+(y-1)^2=4\\) (do not accept \\(2^2\\) for 4)", "(b) M1 — substitutes \\(y=2x+a\\) into their circle; A1 — full expansion shown leading to the given equation (AG)", "(b) M1 — discriminant of the GIVEN equation \\(= 0\\): \\(16(a-2)^2-20(a-1)^2=0\\)", "(b) A1 — \\(a^2+6a-11=0\\) and \\(a=-3\\pm\\sqrt{20}\\) oe exact (isw)"]}, {"id": "w25-p23-q11", "group": "two", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/23", "question": "11"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["8.4"], "topics": ["two circles", "common chord", "exact centre"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>A circle has equation \\(\\;x^2+y^2-25=0\\).<br>A second circle has the same radius as the first circle, and the coordinates of its centre are both positive.<br>The two circles intersect at the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).<br>The line \\(AB\\) has length 6 and is parallel to the line \\(y=-x\\).</p><p>Find the equation of the second circle in the form \\(\\;x^2+y^2+ax+by+c=0\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are constants.&emsp;<b>[5]</b></p>", "tips": ["Draw it! First circle: centre \\(O(0,0)\\), radius 5. The common chord AB (length 6) is shared by both circles.", "Half-chord + radius → distance from each centre to the chord: \\(\\sqrt{5^2-3^2}=4\\). Equal radii → the two centres sit 4 either side: centres are \\(4+4=8\\) apart.", "The line of centres is PERPENDICULAR to the common chord. AB is parallel to \\(y=-x\\), so the centres lie along \\(y=x\\).", "Move \\(8\\) along \\(y=x\\) from the origin: \\(\\left(\\tfrac{8}{\\sqrt2}, \\tfrac{8}{\\sqrt2}\\right) = (4\\sqrt2, 4\\sqrt2)\\) (both positive ✓). Then expand to the required form — exactly."], "solution": ["<p>First circle: centre \\(O(0,0)\\), \\(r=5\\). Distance from O to the chord AB: \\(\\sqrt{25-3^2} = 4\\).</p>", "<p>The second circle has the same radius, so its centre is also at distance 4 from AB, on the other side: the centres are \\(8\\) apart, along the line perpendicular to AB.</p>", "<p>AB \\(\\parallel y=-x\\) → line of centres along \\(y=x\\). Moving 8 from O along \\(y=x\\): centre \\(=\\left(\\tfrac{8}{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac{8}{\\sqrt2}\\right) = (4\\sqrt2, 4\\sqrt2)\\) (both coordinates positive ✓).</p>", "<p>Second circle: \\((x-4\\sqrt2)^2+(y-4\\sqrt2)^2 = 25\\)</p>", "<p>Expand: \\(x^2+y^2-8\\sqrt2\\,x-8\\sqrt2\\,y + 32+32-25 = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2+y^2-8\\sqrt2\\,x-8\\sqrt2\\,y+39=0\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-7, 13], "y": [-7, 13], "xTicks": 5, "yTicks": 5, "curves": [{"circle": [0, 0, 5]}, {"circle": [5.6569, 5.6569, 5], "cls": "c2"}, {"poly": [1, 0], "dashed": true}], "points": [[0, 0, "O"], [5.6569, 5.6569, "(4√2, 4√2)"]]}}], "answer": "\\(x^2+y^2-8\\sqrt2\\,x-8\\sqrt2\\,y+39=0\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — radius 5 soi (or 25 used as \\(r^2\\) in their second equation)", "B2 — centre \\((4\\sqrt2, 4\\sqrt2)\\) soi oe (B1 for (5.66, 5.66) or for distance between centres = 8 soi)", "M1 — forms circle equation with their \\((k,k)\\) centre and \\(r^2=25\\)", "A1 — \\(x^2+y^2-8\\sqrt2x-8\\sqrt2y+39=0\\) (must be exact)"]}, {"id": "s26-p22-q11", "group": "two", "source": {"session": "May/June 2026", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "11"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["8.1", "8.4"], "topics": ["circle through a point", "common chord", "intersection of circles"], "marks": 9, "targetMinutes": 14, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p><i>Solutions to this question by accurate drawing will not be accepted.</i></p><p>A circle \\(C_1\\) has centre \\((3, 5)\\). The point \\(P(1, 4)\\) lies on circle \\(C_1\\).</p><p>A circle \\(C_2\\) has centre \\((12, 8)\\). The line with equation \\(y = 19-3x\\) is the common chord of circles \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\).</p><p>Find the radius of circle \\(C_2\\).&emsp;<b>[9]</b></p>", "figure": {"x": [-3, 22], "y": [-2, 18], "xTicks": 5, "yTicks": 5, "curves": [{"circle": [3, 5, 2.2361]}, {"circle": [12, 8, 8.0623], "cls": "c2"}, {"poly": [-3, 19], "cls": "c1", "dashed": true}], "points": [[3, 5, "(3,5)"], [12, 8, "(12,8)"], [1, 4, "P"], [4, 7, ""], [5, 4, ""]]}, "tips": ["Build \\(C_1\\) first: \\(r_1^2 = (3-1)^2+(5-4)^2 = 5\\), so \\(C_1: (x-3)^2+(y-5)^2 = 5\\).", "The common chord is a real line through BOTH intersection points: substitute \\(y = 19-3x\\) into \\(C_1\\) and solve — you get the two points A and B where the circles cross.", "\\(10x^2 - 90x + 200 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 4, 5\\): intersection points \\((4, 7)\\) and \\((5, 4)\\).", "\\(C_2\\) passes through those same points: \\(r_2^2 = (12-4)^2+(8-7)^2 = 65\\). Final answer \\(\\sqrt{65}\\) — NOT ±√65 (a radius is positive; the scheme gives A0 for ±)."], "solution": ["<p>\\(C_1\\): radius² \\(= (3-1)^2+(5-4)^2 = 5\\), so \\((x-3)^2+(y-5)^2 = 5\\).</p>", "<p>Substitute the chord \\(y = 19-3x\\): \\((x-3)^2 + (14-3x)^2 = 5\\)</p>", "<p>\\(x^2-6x+9 + 9x^2-84x+196 = 5 \\Rightarrow 10x^2 - 90x + 200 = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2-9x+20 = 0 \\Rightarrow (x-4)(x-5) = 0\\).</p>", "<p>Intersection points: \\(A(4, 7)\\) and \\(B(5, 4)\\) (using the chord equation for y).</p>", "<p>These points lie on \\(C_2\\) too: \\(r_2^2 = (12-4)^2 + (8-7)^2 = 64+1 = 65\\) (same from \\((5,4)\\): \\(49+16=65\\)).</p>", "<p>Radius of \\(C_2\\) \\(= \\sqrt{65}\\) \\((\\approx 8.06)\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(r = \\sqrt{65}\\)", "markScheme": ["B2 — forms \\(C_1\\): \\((x-3)^2+(y-5)^2=5\\) oe soi (B1 for \\((x-3)^2+(y-5)^2=k\\) or \\(r^2=5\\) soi)", "M1 — uses \\(y=19-3x\\) with their \\(C_1\\) to eliminate one unknown; A1 — correct unsimplified equation", "A1 — 3-term quadratic \\(10x^2-90x+200[=0]\\) oe; M1 — solves their quadratic", "A1 — \\(x=4, x=5\\); A1 — at least one intersection point \\((4,7)\\) or \\((5,4)\\)", "A1 — \\(r=\\sqrt{65}\\) oe nfww (A0 for final answer ±\\(\\sqrt{65}\\))"]}]});
